WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.900
Chile's 1981 pension privatization sought stronger retirement savings, but instead expanded self-employment

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:14.380
and irregular contributions. This is unintended consequences, episode 105. In the months after

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:20.620
Chile moved its pension system to individual accounts in 1981, formal sector employers began

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:26.140
calculating the new payroll deductions required for each worker. The change was meant to link

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:31.260
contributions directly to future benefits and reduce long-term fiscal pressure on the state.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:38.120
Instead, labor force surveys soon recorded a noticeable rise in self-employment, as workers

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.640
and firms adjusted to the higher cost of remaining in the formal sector. The arithmetic was

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:49.340
straightforward on paper. A larger slice of each wage now flowed into privately managed accounts

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:54.700
rather than a collective pool, yet that same increase raised the total expense attached to

00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:00.400
keeping any single employee inside the formal payroll. Employers therefore faced a recurring

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.140
choice between absorbing the added cost or restructuring the relationship, so the mandate no longer

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:12.040
applied in full. Workers, seeing the same numbers on their pay stubs, weighed the security of

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.940
steady formal coverage against the flexibility of arrangements where contributions could be skipped

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:23.900
or reduced without immediate penalty. Chilean policymakers in the late 1970s confronted an older

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:29.340
pay-as-you-go pension arrangement that many viewed as financially strained and poorly linked to

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:35.120
individual effort. Shifting to mandatory individual accounts appeared to offer a cleaner solution.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:41.060
Workers would own their savings, returns would reflect market performance, and the state would face

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:47.160
clearer limits on future obligations. The approach aligned with a broader effort to modernize labor and

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:53.360
capital markets while encouraging personal responsibility for retirement. At the time, the designers

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.740
expected that the direct connection between contributions and benefits would raise overall saving rates

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:04.600
and improve replacement ratios for those who participated steadily. The reform therefore treated

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:10.300
formal employment as the default setting in which these incentives would operate. They reasoned that

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:15.960
once people saw their own balances grow with each deposit, the habit of regular payment would become

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:22.980
self-reinforcing, and the old problem of diffuse political claims on a common fund would recede. Because the

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:29.120
prior system had already shown signs of strain under demographic and fiscal pressure, the new architecture

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:35.560
looked like a way to restore credibility without expanding state promises. The new system took effect

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:42.400
in 1981, requiring employers and employees in the formal sector to direct a fixed percentage of

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:48.740
wages into privately managed accounts. Early official statements highlighted rising participation numbers

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:53.260
and the administrative machinery that had been built to track individual balances.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:59.160
Proponents pointed to the transparent link between what a worker paid in and what would later be

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:05.100
received. Some observers noted that the contribution rate itself had increased relative to the prior

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:10.720
regime, yet the prevailing view was that workers would accept the higher deduction once they saw the

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:16.460
ownership feature of the accounts. Labor market data collection continued through national surveys,

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:22.520
providing the first indications of how employment patterns responded. The rollout emphasized the

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:28.300
creation of new private fund managers and the transfer of existing balances, steps that required

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:34.620
precise coordination between government agencies and the emerging financial sector. Because the mandate

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:40.400
applied only where formal contracts already existed, the architects assumed the boundary of coverage

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.480
would remain stable, while the quality of participation inside that boundary improved.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.500
The higher payroll burden attached to formal contracts prompted many workers and small firms to

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:57.540
reorganize employment relationships so that the mandate no longer applied. Self-employment rates

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:03.960
climbed in the following decades, documented consistently in labor force surveys, as people moved into

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:10.380
categories where contribution requirements were lighter or easier to avoid. Those who became self-employed

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:16.400
often contributed only intermittently because payments were now voluntary or tied to irregular income.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:21.860
As a result, the average density of contributions fell for a growing share of the workforce,

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:28.360
directly lowering projected replacement rates at retirement. The boundary between formal and informal

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:34.240
activity shifted. Activities that had once been carried out inside firms were reclassified,

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.820
expanding the uncovered population even as the pension architecture assumed steady formal

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:46.280
participation. Second order effects appeared in hiring practices, with firms favoring contractual

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:51.700
arrangements that minimized fixed payroll costs. Over time, the pool of workers with low

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:56.260
contribution histories grew large enough to affect aggregate pension adequacy metrics.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.320
The original design had treated the formal sector as stable. Once that assumption was challenged

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:08.280
by the cost increase, the system produced more uncovered workers than anticipated. A further

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:12.980
layer emerged when intermittent contributors reached retirement age and discovered that even

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:18.780
modest gaps in payment history translated into sharply reduced monthly benefits, prompting greater

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:25.120
reliance on minimum guarantees or family support. This feedback loop reinforced the attractiveness

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:30.520
of remaining outside formal contracts, because the perceived return on consistent payment looked

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:35.940
lower when measured against actual outcomes for people with variable earnings. Subsequent

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:40.980
governments introduced adjustments aimed at encouraging voluntary contributions from self-employed

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:47.080
workers and at strengthening enforcement were possible. Some measures added subsidies or simplified

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:53.260
payment channels, yet participation rates among the newly self-employed remained uneven. Later

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:59.240
reforms revisited contribution mandates and introduced minimum guarantees, but these changes themselves

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:05.200
required further calibration as labor markets continued to evolve. Today, the system retains its

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.540
individual account structure while operating alongside targeted programs that address gaps in

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:16.080
coverage. The experience has informed ongoing discussions about how contribution rules interact

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:21.240
with the composition of employment. Each round of adjustment has had to balance the original

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:27.100
goal of preserving individual ownership against the practical reality that many workers now operate

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:33.060
in arrangements where steady payroll deduction is structurally difficult. Incentive structures often

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:38.880
redirect activity toward the least costly compliance path, rather than the intended one. When a

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:44.780
mandate raises the price of remaining in one category of work, rational actors explore alternatives

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:50.820
that fall outside the mandate's reach. Designers who focus narrowly on the formal sector may therefore

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:56.820
enlarge the very group whose behavior they hoped to change. Proposals that assume a fixed boundary

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:02.400
between covered and uncovered work can produce the opposite result once the cost of staying inside

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:08.520
that boundary rises. How might today's proposals for platform workers or gig economy contributors

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:13.940
account for similar boundary effects before rules are locked in? That's the case for today.

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:19.300
The lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone who'd find it

